a major apotropaic symbol that ‘kills’ the Minoan dragon and offers a coherent narrative reading. Other cited material (including AI‑mediated but explicitly 
sourced summaries of CMS I, 179) presents alternative interpretations involving a female deity (possibly Demeter), an eagle of Zeus, four seasonal elements, and
sun–moon background, relating the imagery to divinity, nature, cyclical time, and eternity. Additional evidence states that the ring functions as a Mycenaean 
heirloom (κειμήλιο) tied to ancestral respect and cultural identity. Scholarship also stresses methodological difficulty in interpreting such rings due to 
limited archaeological, mythological, and historical context for MH and early LH periods, framing interpretations as cautious and imaginative.", 
"low_gain_streak": 0, "last_evaluated_successful_rounds": 3, "text": "Summarize scholarly interpretations of the Ring of Nestor and its significance, including 
