[tools:b6df3982-239b-f0da-1ce7-d6522188c225] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 2, 'title': 'Description and physical characteristics', 
'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is described in the standard corpus of Aegean glyptic, the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel (CMS), under the numbers 
CMS VI, no. 277 and CMS I, 179, where it is classified as a large gold signet ring belonging to the broader Creto-Mycenaean tradition.[5][6][8] The ring is made
of gold and carries an engraved bezel with a complex figural scene in intaglio, intended to produce a raised impression when used as a seal.[5][7] Ingo 
Pini\x19s study in the Oxford Journal of Archaeology discusses the ring in the context of Mycenaean signet rings from the early phases of the Late Bronze Age, 
aligning it with other high-status pieces dated to the 16th–15th century BC on stylistic grounds.[6][8] A. G. Vlachopoulos similarly places the piece in the 
early Mycenaean horizon while highlighting elements that reflect a Creto-Mycenaean stylistic blend.[5]\n\nScholars describe the ring as unusually large for a 
signet, with a broad bezel that allows for a multi-figure composition and fine detail in the depiction of figures, vegetation and subsidiary motifs.[5][7] The 
gold band appears to have been shaped to accommodate the weight and size of the bezel, suggesting that the ring may have functioned principally as an emblem of 
status or ritual authority rather than as a practical sealing device.[5][7] Discussions of the ring in the CMS volumes and subsequent studies emphasise the 
quality of the engraving, the careful execution of anatomical details and textile patterns, and the arrangement of the scene in horizontal registers or zones 
that guide the viewer\x19s eye across the composition.[5][7][8]\n\nWhile the available publications do not provide a full set of metrological data, the ring is 
consistently presented as a substantial gold object whose dimensions exceed those of many contemporary signet rings, contributing to its prominence in the 
scholarly literature on Mycenaean glyptic.[5][6][7] Its classification in the CMS, combined with stylistic analysis, underpins the attribution of the piece to a
Creto-Mycenaean workshop in the early Late Bronze Age, even though precise workshop location and production context remain topics of discussion.[5][8]', 
'source_ids': ['20260719123159725640_c132fd5c', '20260719123159744730_76b2ce06', '20260719123159753525_f8e85c4c', '20260719123159761182_7bbd158c'], 
'evidence_ids': [], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 'section_type': 'body'})
