[tools:b6df3982-239b-f0da-1ce7-d6522188c225] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 4, 'title': 'Discovery and archaeological context', 
'content': 'The published scholarship on the Ring of Nestor provides only limited information about the circumstances of its discovery and early history. Ingo 
Pini\x19s article on the ring, as well as subsequent discussions of CMS I, 179, treat the piece in connection with the so-called Tiryns treasure, linking it to 
material said to have been found at or near the site of Tiryns in the early twentieth century.[6][8] References to a 1915 discovery by Apostolos Arvanitopoulos 
appear in this context, but the available evidence does not include a detailed primary excavation report that would securely document the tomb, hoard or 
assemblage in which the ring was found.[8]\n\nBecause of these gaps, authors who analyse the Ring of Nestor emphasise that its archaeological context is not 
firmly established.[5][6][8] The ring is sometimes associated in secondary literature with broader discussions of early Mycenaean elite burials and treasures 
from sites such as Tiryns and Pylos, but these associations rely on comparisons of style and iconography rather than on explicit statements that the ring formed
part of a particular grave or hoard.[5][8] The Pylos Regional Archaeological Project and related publications on the so-called Griffin Warrior tomb and nearby 
tholos burials at Ano Englianos have drawn attention to richly furnished shaft graves containing multiple gold signet rings, yet the sources documenting those 
excavations do not identify any of the rings as the \x1cRing of Nestor\x1d or as CMS I, 179.[10][12][13][14][15][16][18]\n\nAs a result, modern discussions 
often distinguish between the secure archaeological contexts of well-documented excavations and the more fragile reconstructions of provenance for pieces like 
the Ring of Nestor, whose early history depends on later cataloguing and museum records.[5][6][8] Some scholars note that uncertainty about the discovery 
context has implications for how the ring is used in broader historical arguments about regional workshops, patterns of elite exchange or the development of 
Mycenaean religious imagery, and they advocate a cautious approach that acknowledges these limitations.[5][6]', 'source_ids': ['20260719123159725640_c132fd5c', 
